---
import Callout from '../callouts/Callout.astro';
import MathBlock from '../patterns/MathBlock.astro';
---
<section id="solver-notes" class="section-grid">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Technical notes</p>
    <h2>Why reversal is interesting</h2>
    <p>
      Forward simulation is cheap. Reversal is not. The constraint system has to explain every cell
      in generation <code>t + 1</code> in terms of its neighbors in generation <code>t</code>,
      which turns a simple local rule into a global search problem.
    </p>
  </div>

  <MathBlock
    expression="\mathrm{alive}_{t+1}(x, y) \iff \left(\mathrm{alive}_{t}(x, y) \land n_t(x, y) \in \{2,3\}\right) \lor \left(\neg \mathrm{alive}_{t}(x, y) \land n_t(x, y)=3\right)"
    caption="The forward rule is compact. The search space for valid predecessors is not."
  />

  <Callout title="What the browser demo proves" variant="info">
    <p>
      The playground is intentionally scoped to forward evolution, state editing, and pattern import/export.
      The reverse solver stays outside the browser because the interesting work is in SAT encoding and solver strategy,
      not in pretending every expensive operation belongs client-side.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="notes-grid">
    <article class="surface-panel notes-card">
      <h3>Engine track</h3>
      <p>
        The performance work lives in grid layout, bit packing, SIMD-aware operations, and keeping the hot path tight
        enough that WASM remains responsive even with richer browser controls.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="surface-panel notes-card">
      <h3>Solver track</h3>
      <p>
        The reverse side benefits more from clean encodings than from forcing clever heuristics too early. Clause shape,
        pruning, and validation were more important than exotic frontend gimmicks.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
.notes-grid {
  display: grid;
  gap: 1rem;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
}

.notes-card {
  padding: 1.2rem;
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.75rem;
}
</style>
